Nat Cell Biol</source><year>2001</year><volume>3</volume><fpage>897</fpage><lpage>904</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11584271</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b36"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sodeik</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanisms of viral transport in the cytoplasm</article-title><source>Trends Microbiol</source><year>2000</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>465</fpage><lpage>472</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11044681</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b37"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ward</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Visualization and characterization of the intracellular movement of vaccinia virus intracellular mature virions</article-title><source>J Virol</source><year>2005</year><volume>79</volume><fpage>4755</fpage><lpage>4763</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15795261</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b38"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ward</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Visualization of intracellular movement of vaccinia virus virions containing a green fluorescent protein-B5R membrane protein chimera</article-title><source>J Virol</source><year>2001a</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>4802</fpage><lpage>4813</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11312352</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b39"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ward</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Vaccinia virus intracellular movement is associated with microtubules and independent of actin tails</article-title><source>J Virol</source><year>2001b</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>11651</fpage><lpage>11663</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11689647</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b40"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ward</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Vaccinia virus A36R membrane protein provides a direct link between intracellular enveloped virions and the microtubule motor kinesin</article-title><source>J Virol</source><year>2004</year><volume>78</volume><fpage>2486</fpage><lpage>2493</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14963148</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b41"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ward</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Weisberg</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mapping and functional analysis of interaction sites within the cytoplasmic domains of the vaccinia virus A33R and A36R envelope proteins</article-title><source>J Virol</source><year>2003</year><volume>77</volume><fpage>4113</fpage><lpage>4126</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12634370</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b42"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weisswange</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Schleich</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Newsome</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Way</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The rate of N-WASP exchange limits the extent of Arp2/3 complex dependent actin-based motility</article-title><source>Nature</source><comment>in press</comment></citation></ref><ref id="b43"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wolffe</surname><given-names>EJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Weisberg</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Role for the vaccinia virus A36R outer envelope protein in the formation of virus-tipped actin-containing microvilli and cell-to-cell virus spread</article-title><source>Virology</source><year>1998</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>20</fpage><lpage>26</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9581774</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b44"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wolffe</surname><given-names>EJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Weisberg</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Moss</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The vaccinia virus A33R protein provides a chaperone function for viral membrane localization and tyrosine phosphorylation of the A36R protein</article-title><source>J Virol</source><year>2001</year><volume>75</volume><fpage>303</fpage><lpage>310</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11119600</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b45"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zettl</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Way</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The WH1 and EVH1 domains of WASP and Ena/VASP family members bind distinct sequence motifs</article-title><source>Curr Biol</source><year>2002</year><volume>12</volume><fpage>1617</fpage><lpage>1622</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12372256</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b46"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name><name><surname>Wilcock</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>GL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Vaccinia virus F12L protein is required for actin tail formation, normal plaque size, and virulence</article-title><source>J Virol</source><year>2000</year><volume>74</volume><fpage>11654</fpage><lpage>11662</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11090164</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supporting Information</title><p>Additional Supporting Information may be found in the online version of this article:</p><p><bold>Fig. S1.</bold> GFP-F12 is associated with all IEV. Immunofluorescence images of WR-GFP-F12-infected HeLa cells showing that GFP-F12 is associated with all intracellular F13-positive virus particles. Pink arrows indicate IEV that are F13-, DAPI- and GFP-F12-positive but negative for extracellular B5. White arrows indicate CEV that are positive for F13, DAPI and extracellular B5 but negative for GFP-F12. Yellow arrow points to a F13-positive vesicle that lacks signals from DAPI, GFP-F12 and extracellular B5. Orange arrow shows IMV (DAPI-positive only). The scale bar is 5 &#x003bc;m.</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD1"><media xlink:href="cmi0011-0808-SD1.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1cea180N0x2264030" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><p><bold>Fig. S2.</bold> E2 and F12 are required for IEV morphogenesis. Electron micrographs showing the peri-nuclear wrapping compartment in &#x00394;E2L- and &#x00394;F12L-infected HeLa cells at 8 h post infection. Red arrows show examples of partially wrapped IMV. Scale bars = 0.1 &#x003bc;m top left panel and 0.5 &#x003bc;m all other panels.</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD2"><media xlink:href="cmi0011-0808-SD2.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1cea180N0x22640f0" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><p><bold>Fig. S3.</bold> E2 and F12 are required for IEV morphogenesis in BSC-1 Cells. Electron micrographs showing the peri-nuclear wrapping compartment in &#x00394;E2L- and &#x00394;F12L-infected BSC-1 cells at 10 h post infection. Red arrows highlight examples of partially wrapped IMV. Scale bars = 0.5 &#x003bc;m.</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD3"><media xlink:href="cmi0011-0808-SD3.pdf" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1cea180N0x22641b0" position="anchor" mimetype="application" mime-subtype="pdf"/></supplementary-material><p><bold>Movie S1.</bold> GFP-F12 is associated with IEV undergoing rapid linear movement towards the cell periphery. Left panel = GFPF12, middle = RFP-A3, right = Merge. Yellow arrow highlights an IEV (positive for GFP-F12 and RFP-A3) moving from the site of wrapping towards the cell periphery. Red arrow shows a virus (presumably IMV) moving in the absence of F12. Time in seconds and scale bar are indicated in the left panel.</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD4"><media xlink:href="cmi0011-0808-SD4.mov" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1cea180N0x2264270" position="anchor" mimetype="video" mime-subtype="quicktime"/></supplementary-material><p><bold>Movie S2.</bold> The movie shows the loss of GFP-F12 during the transition from microtubule to actin-based motility. Left panel = GFP-F12, middle = RFP-A3, right = Merge. Yellow arrow highlights an IEV (positive for GFP-F12 and RFP-A3) moving towards the cell periphery. The arrow changes from yellow to red as F12 vanishes and the virus switches to a slower more processive movement. Time in seconds and scale bar are indicated in the left panel.</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD5"><media xlink:href="cmi0011-0808-SD5.mov" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1cea180N0x2264330" position="anchor" mimetype="video" mime-subtype="quicktime"/></supplementary-material><p><bold>Movie S3.</bold> The arrowhead indicates an IEV (B5-YFP- and RFPA3- positive) undergoing rapid linear movements in the absence of F12. Left panel = B5-YFP, middle = RFP-A3, right = Merge. Time in seconds and scale bar are indicated.</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD6"><media xlink:href="cmi0011-0808-SD6.mov" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1cea180N0x22643f0" position="anchor" mimetype="video" mime-subtype="quicktime"/></supplementary-material><p><bold>Movie S4.</bold> The movie shows the loss of GFP-E2 during the transition from microtubule to actin-based motility. Left panel = GFP-E2, middle = RFP-A3, right = Merge. Yellow arrow highlights an IEV (positive for GFP-E2 and RFP-A3) moving towards the cell periphery. The arrow changes from yellow to red as E2 vanishes and the virus switches to a slower more processive movement. Time in seconds and scale bar are indicated in the left panel.</p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="SD7"><media xlink:href="cmi0011-0808-SD7.mov" xlink:type="simple" id="N0x1cea180N0x22644b0" position="anchor" mimetype="video" mime-subtype="quicktime"/></supplementary-material><p>Please note: Wiley-Blackwell are not responsible for the content or functionality of any supporting materials supplied by the authors. Any queries (other than missing material) should be directed to the corresponding author for the article.</p></sec></back></article>